A `.mtf` file has a small compile-time scope. It sees the fixed HTML prelude and can select declarations from same-module helper files with qualified references. It has no implicit access to module root declarations.

### Same-module helpers

An ordinary `.moth` helper declaration is reachable from a `.mtf` file in the same module through a qualified reference:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- docs/settings.moth
    site_name #= "Moth"
]

[codeblock, $code "md":
    -- docs/intro.mtf
    # Welcome to [@settings site_name]
]

The provider path names the helper file, the selected symbol names one compile-time declaration and the two tokens stay on the same line. The template folds the selected value during compilation. The directory needs no `@mod.moth` file for this to work.

A qualified reference introduces no local binding. It therefore never shadows a prelude name or another declaration. Selecting `@home_constants p` produces that helper's `p`, even though the prelude also exposes `p`.

### Root-local declarations stay hidden

Declarations authored directly in `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` or `@entry.moth` belong to that root file only. A `.mtf` file cannot read them, even when the facade exports the declaration:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- docs/@mod.moth
    $export
    site_name #= "Moth"
]

[codeblock, $code "md":
    -- docs/intro.mtf
    # Welcome to [site_name]
]

The unqualified name fails with a root-local visibility diagnostic instead of resolving through the facade. Naming the root file directly also fails, because root files are not qualified-reference providers.

Move the shared value into an ordinary helper file when a template needs it. A helper declaration is shared by every root and selectable from the template.

### The fixed HTML prelude

Exported compile-time constants from `@html` are visible flat inside every `.mtf` body. This includes helpers such as `p`, `codeblock` and other HTML constants.

[codeblock, $code "text":
    -- docs/intro.mtf
    [p: This paragraph uses the @html p helper.]

    [codeblock, $code "moth": value = 42]
]

The prelude is fixed compiler policy. It always resolves to the official `packages/html/@mod.moth` source package with its Builder origin and MothSource backing intact. A builder that enables `.mtf` sources must register that official package set. `@html` is reserved for it. Source packages that contain `.mtf` files order after `@html`.

The prelude supplies compile-time constants only. It is not a general template-wide import mechanism and it never includes arbitrary builder-registered constants.

### Collision semantics

A real local binding that matches a prelude name fails with an ordinary visible-name collision. The diagnostic identifies both sources where location data is available.

A qualified selection creates no local binding, so it cannot collide merely because the selected name matches a prelude constant:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- docs/home_constants.moth
    p #= "local p"
]

[codeblock, $code "text":
    -- docs/intro.mtf
    [p][@home_constants p]
]

The first `p` comes from the prelude and the second from the helper. Both render.

### Nested templates

Nested templates inside a `.mtf` body are valid. A nested template with no explicit directive defaults to `$md`. Any explicit directive (such as `$raw`, `$fresh`, `$html`, `$code`, `$css` or `$escape_html`) overrides the moth template Markdown default for that nested template.

[codeblock, $code "text":
    [$raw: <div>Raw HTML output</div>]
]

### Constants versus runtime values

Qualified references inside a `.mtf` file must resolve to compile-time constants. Runtime bindings, runtime functions, types, traits, methods, external runtime APIs and the generated self `content` constant are not valid template values.

A reference to a runtime value is rejected with `MOTH-RULE-0034` or `MOTH-RULE-0053`.

### No runtime capture

Moth template bodies are const-required. There is no runtime capture. The entire body must fold at compile time.

### Allowed template directives

Formatting and child-selection directives available in the compile-time template environment are valid inside `.mtf`. This includes `$md`, `$raw`, `$fresh`, `$html`, `$code`, `$css` and `$escape_html`.

A `.mtf` file produces one fixed `content` constant. It cannot expose its own slot receiver or contribute a builder root slot.

### Child-template formatting boundary

Child templates are opaque barriers to the parent formatter. A child template's output is not inspected by the parent `$md` formatter. Template delimiters cannot pair across child boundaries.

### Literal delimiter output

To output literal template delimiters, use string insertion:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    ["["]
    ["]"]
    ["[literal]"]
]

### Content helpers

Content helpers such as `codeblock` come from the fixed `@html` prelude above, not from the active builder. A `.mtf` file can also name an ordinary helper with an explicit qualified reference. It does not gain that helper's declarations automatically.

### Concepts related to template scope

- For the full template directive list see @../templates/#template-directives (Template directives).
- For `$md` formatting see @../templates/#markdown-formatting (Markdown formatting).
- For the `.mtf` content constant see @#content-dependencies (Content dependencies).
- For limitations see @#moth-template-files-limits (Moth template limits).
